              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">450&nbsp;&deg;F</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">230&nbsp;&deg;C</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">210&nbsp;&deg;C</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Very hot, blistering pie pastry</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">9</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">475&nbsp;&deg;F</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">240&nbsp;&deg;C</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">220&nbsp;&deg;C</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Meringue browning, naan, demand settings</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        A fan oven moves that number down, because moving air transfers heat faster than still air.
        The working rule on most appliance dials is 20&nbsp;&deg;C or 25&nbsp;&deg;F lower than the
        conventional figure, with a shorter bake: start checking about a quarter of the way before the
        recipe's time. That is a starting point, not a law. The pause in rising, the colour, and above
        all the internal temperature are what decide whether it is done.
        <a href="/tools/cooking-time" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">The cooking time
        calculator</a> converts a conventional time and weight into a starting estimate, and
        <a href="/tools/bread-proofing" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">the bread proofing
        guide</a> covers the dough stages where oven temperature matters most.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-4">
        Some oven temperatures are a safety limit, not a preference
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The oven setting browns food. It does not by itself tell you the food is safe, because the
        inside of a roast heats far more slowly than the air around it. The U.S. Department of
        Agriculture publishes minimum internal temperatures instead, measured with a food thermometer
        once the food comes out of the oven and, for whole cuts, held through a rest. These are the
        numbers to cook to when the dish contains meat, poultry, seafood or eggs.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[720px] border-collapse text-left text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
          <caption class="px-4 pb-2 text-left text-sm font-medium text-[var(--text-muted)]">
            Safe minimum internal temperatures published by USDA FSIS and FoodSafety.gov.
          </caption>
