            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Ground meats</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">160&nbsp;&deg;F (71.1&nbsp;&deg;C)</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">All poultry, including ground poultry and stuffing</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">165&nbsp;&deg;F (73.9&nbsp;&deg;C)</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Ham, fresh or smoked and uncooked</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">145&nbsp;&deg;F (62.8&nbsp;&deg;C)</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">3 minutes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Fully cooked ham, reheated</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">140&nbsp;&deg;F (60&nbsp;&deg;C) if packed in a USDA-inspected plant, otherwise 165&nbsp;&deg;F</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Eggs and egg dishes</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">160&nbsp;&deg;F (71.1&nbsp;&deg;C)</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Fish and shellfish</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">145&nbsp;&deg;F (62.8&nbsp;&deg;C)</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Leftovers and casseroles</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">165&nbsp;&deg;F (73.9&nbsp;&deg;C)</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">None</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Two details get missed. The rest time for whole cuts is part of the cooking, not a serving
        nicety: the temperature keeps rising and the juices redistribute while the joint stands, which
        is why the minimum applies at the end of the rest as well as the moment it leaves the oven.
        And the range between 40&nbsp;&deg;F and 140&nbsp;&deg;F is the zone where bacteria multiply
        fastest, so an oven held below 140&nbsp;&deg;F is not cooking food, it is holding it in that
        zone. If you are keeping a dish warm for a buffet,
        <a href="/tools/potluck-coordinator" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">the potluck
        coordinator</a> sets out the holding limits, and
        <a href="/tools/meal-prep" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">the meal prep
        planner</a> covers what happens to the food after it cools.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-4">
        Check the oven, not the dial
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        An oven thermostat cycles on and off around its set point, so the display is a target rather
        than a measurement. Kansas State University's extension calibration guide defines an accurate
        thermometer as one reading within plus or minus 2&nbsp;&deg;F (0.5&nbsp;&deg;C) of the true
        temperature, and notes that dial thermometers lose calibration after physical shock. An
        inexpensive oven thermometer left on the middle rack for twenty minutes tells you what your
        oven is actually doing, and it is the cheapest fix for a cake that browns before it sets.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        You can check that thermometer at home against two fixed points. A slush of crushed ice and
        water reads 32&nbsp;&deg;F (0&nbsp;&deg;C). Boiling water reads 212&nbsp;&deg;F
        (100&nbsp;&deg;C) at sea level, and less as you climb, because the boiling point falls with
        air pressure. The USDA figures reproduced in the K-State guide give the correction directly.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] border-collapse text-left text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
          <caption class="px-4 pb-2 text-left text-sm font-medium text-[var(--text-muted)]">
            Boiling point of water by altitude, from the USDA Complete Guide to Home Canning (Agriculture
            Information Bulletin No. 539), as reproduced by Kansas State University extension.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <th class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--text-primary)]">Altitude</th>
