            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        If the oven thermometer disagrees with the dial by more than about 25&nbsp;&deg;F across the
        range, stop trusting the dial and use the measured figure in recipes. If a probe thermometer
        cannot be calibrated and reads more than 2&nbsp;&deg;F out, the K-State guidance is to replace
        it rather than adjust around it. Two smaller habits close the same gap: preheat until the
        element has cycled off rather than until the beep, and bake in the middle of the oven, where
        the air is most even, on the rack the recipe names.
        <a href="/tools/unit-converter" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">The unit
        converter</a> handles weights and volumes when a recipe mixes systems, and
        <a href="/tools/sourdough-calculator" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">the sourdough
        calculator</a> scales a formula to the loaf you actually want to bake.
      </p>

      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Sources:
        <a href="https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart" rel="noopener" class="underline">USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, &ldquo;Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart&rdquo; (last updated 14 April 2025)</a>;
        <a href="https://www.foodsafety.gov/food-safety-charts/safe-minimum-internal-temperatures" rel="noopener" class="underline">FoodSafety.gov, &ldquo;Cook to a Safe Minimum Internal Temperature&rdquo; (reviewed 21 November 2024)</a>;
        <a href="https://www.asi.k-state.edu/doc/meat-science/thermometer-calibration-guide-2.pdf" rel="noopener" class="underline">Flores and Boyle, &ldquo;Thermometer Calibration Guide&rdquo;, Kansas State University Agricultural Experiment Station and Cooperative Extension Service, MF-2440</a>.
      </p>
